.TH TAKE 1C UniSoft
.SH NAME
take \- takes a file from a remote machine
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B take
[
.B \-p
.I port
] [
.BR \-s SPEED
] [
.B \-i
[ ID ] ]
.I fromfile
[
.I tofile
]
.br
.B take
[
.B \-p
.I port
] [
.BR \-s SPEED
]
.B \-c
.I command
[
.I args
] ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Take\^
is part of system of programs useful for transferring files between 
.SM UNIX
systems.  It is the "downloader" designed to transmit files from a remote
machine to a local machine.  For a brief discussion of the take/put
system and installation instructions, see the companion document:
\fIInstallation and Overview of the UniSoft Take/Put File Transfer System\fR.
.PP
.I Take\^
transfers a file, directory, or output from a command given at a remote 
machine.
The default
.I port\^
is 
.BR /dev/tty0 ;
the 
.B \-p
option can be used to specify an alternate port.
The default
.I speed\^
is determined by the system; the
.B \-s
option can be used to specify a specific speed.
The 
.B \-i
[ 
.SM ID 
]
option remaps pathnames on the remote machine.
The
.B 
.SM ID
(if present) is passed to the remote machine where
it is used to locate a line containing pathname prefixes (using the
.B /etc/takelist
file discussed below).
If no
.B 
.SM ID
is given after the
.B \-i
flag, then the default system 
.SM ID
is read from the
.B /etc/sys_id
file (if it exists); otherwise
.B take7
will use the account name of the invoker (i.e., the person who logged in to
the port used) to determine which line of
.B /etc/takelist
to apply.
See the overview document for details of the mapping.
.PP
The
.B \-c
option is useful for executing an arbitrary command on the remote machine.
All arguments following the
.B \-c
flag are collected, transmitted to the remote machine and executed as a single
command.  The standard output and standard error from this command are
returned as the standard output and standard error of
.BR take .
.PP
In order to perform its function,
.IR take\^ (1C)
interfaces with the program 
.B /usr/bin/take7
on the remote machine.
.SH EXAMPLE
.IP
take /a/b/c
.PP
takes the contents of the directory (or file) "/a/b/c" on the remote machine
and copies them into a similarly named directory (or file) on the
local machine; if "/a/b/c" did not previously exist on the local
machine, it is created; otherwise it is overwritten.
.IP
take file.c /x/y/z/filename
.PP
takes the contents of "file.c" from the remote machine and copies
them into "/x/y/z/filename" on the local machine.  Note that
if "filename"
exists on the local machine, 
its contents are overwritten.
.SH FILES
.TP "\w'fromfile\ \ \ \ 'u"
fromfile
The remote file name.  When using the
.B \-i
option, 
this file should usually be specified as a pathname starting at the root
of the local machine.
.ns
.TP
tofile
The local file name; if 
.I tofile\^
is null,
.I tofile\^
is defaulted to
.IR fromfile .
If 
.I tofile\^
is a directory, then
.I tofile\^
has the last segment of the
.I fromfile\^
path appended to it.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
cu(1C), put(1C)
.br
.I "Installation and Overview of the UniSoft Take/Put File Transfer System"
.\"	@(#)take.1	5.2 of 7/23/84
